# Research Request: SolidJS CSS Animation Not Playing on First Keyed Remount (Two-Phase rAF Failing in Tests)

## The Objective & Core Issue

I'm building a tabbed catalog page in SolidJS (SolidJS 1.9 + Vite + TailwindCSS v4 + `@kobalte/core` Tabs). When the user switches tabs, I want the new tab panel to slide in directionally (left/right depending on tab order), similar to page-transition slide animations.

The bug: **The slide animation does not play on the FIRST tab change after initial render, but DOES play on the second and subsequent changes.** This is the classic "browser skips the animation because the element is created with the animation class already applied — the browser never observes a state transition from 'no class' to 'has class'" problem.

I attempted a two-phase fix: mount the element WITHOUT the animation class, then add the class via `requestAnimationFrame` so the browser observes the class change. My tests for this two-phase behavior are failing, and I need to understand both (a) why the tests fail and (b) whether this is the correct approach in SolidJS at all.

## Relevant Code Snippets

### 1. The tab slide CSS keyframes (theme.css)
```css
/* registered as Tailwind v4 @theme animate tokens */
--animate-tab-slide-right: tab-slide-right 0.25s var(--ease-standard);
--animate-tab-slide-left: tab-slide-left 0.25s var(--ease-standard);

@keyframes tab-slide-right {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(32px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); }
}
@keyframes tab-slide-left {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-32px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); }
}
```

### 2. The catalog page (src/pages/catalog/index.tsx) — CURRENT BROKEN STATE
```tsx
import { createSignal, Show, type JSX } from "solid-js";
import { SubPageShell } from "~/components/layout/sub-page-shell/sub-page-shell";
import {
  Tabs,
  TabsIndicator,
  TabsList,
  TabsTrigger,
} from "~/components/ui/tabs";
import { CategoryTab } from "./components/category-tab";
import { ProductTab } from "./components/product-tab";
import { SpeedDialFab } from "./components/speed-dial-fab";
import { VariantTab } from "./components/variant-tab";

const TAB_ORDER = ["produk", "varian", "kategori"] as const;
type TabKey = (typeof TAB_ORDER)[number];

export default function CatalogPage() {
  const [tab, setTab] = createSignal<TabKey>("produk");
  const [direction, setDirection] = createSignal<"left" | "right">("right");

  const handleTabChange = (value: string) => {
    const newIndex = TAB_ORDER.indexOf(value as TabKey);
    const oldIndex = TAB_ORDER.indexOf(tab());
    setDirection(newIndex >= oldIndex ? "right" : "left");
    setTab(value as TabKey);
  };

  // Two-phase animation: mount element WITHOUT animation class,
  // then add it via requestAnimationFrame so the browser sees
  // a state change and reliably triggers the CSS animation.
  const applySlide = (el: HTMLElement) => {
    const dir = direction();
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      el.classList.add(
        dir === "right" ? "animate-tab-slide-right" : "animate-tab-slide-left",
      );
    });
  };

  return (
    <SubPageShell backHref="/" data-ssgoi-transition="/catalog" title="Katalog">
      <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col overflow-hidden">
        <Tabs
          class="flex flex-1 flex-col overflow-hidden"
          onChange={handleTabChange}
          value={tab()}
        >
          <div class="relative shrink-0 border-border border-b">
            <TabsList class="relative flex w-full">
              <TabsTrigger class="flex-1" value="produk">Produk</TabsTrigger>
              <TabsTrigger class="flex-1" value="varian">Varian</TabsTrigger>
              <TabsTrigger class="flex-1" value="kategori">Kategori</TabsTrigger>
              <TabsIndicator class="bg-primary" />
            </TabsList>
          </div>

          {/* Keyed remount on tab change → directive adds animation via rAF */}
          <div class="relative flex-1 overflow-hidden">
            <Show keyed when={tab()}>
              {() => (
                <div ref={applySlide} class="h-full overflow-hidden">
                  <Show when={tab() === "produk"}><ProductTab /></Show>
                  <Show when={tab() === "varian"}><VariantTab /></Show>
                  <Show when={tab() === "kategori"}><CategoryTab /></Show>
                </div>
              )}
            </Show>
          </div>
        </Tabs>
      </div>

      <SpeedDialFab />
    </SubPageShell>
  );
}
```

Note: earlier I tried `use:slideIn` (Solid custom directive) instead of `ref={applySlide}` — same test failures. Before that I tried applying the animation class directly via `cn()` reactive class binding (no rAF), which had the real-browser bug (first change no animation) but passed the class-presence tests.

### 3. The failing test file (src/pages/catalog/__test__/tab-animation.test.tsx)
```tsx
// matchMedia mock + ResizeObserver mock + rAF mock setup omitted for brevity.
// rAF is mocked to capture callbacks in rafCallbacks[] without running them.
// flushRaf() runs them. SubPageShell is mocked to a passthrough div.

describe("CatalogPage tab slide animation", () => {
  it("does NOT apply slide class at mount — only after rAF (two-phase)", async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup();
    const CatalogPage = (await import("../index")).default;
    render(() => <CatalogPage />);

    await user.click(screen.getByText("Varian"));

    const panel = screen.getByTestId("variant-panel").parentElement;

    // Phase 1: at mount, no slide class yet
    expect(panel?.className).not.toContain("slide");   // <-- PASSES with current code

    // Phase 2: after rAF flush, slide class appears
    flushRaf();
    expect(panel?.className).toContain("slide");       // <-- PASSES
    expect(panel?.className).toContain("right");       // <-- PASSES
  });

  it("applies slide-left after rAF when moving to a lower tab", async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup();
    const CatalogPage = (await import("../index")).default;
    render(() => <CatalogPage />);

    // Go to kategori first (rightward)
    await user.click(screen.getByText("Kategori"));
    flushRaf();

    // Then back to produk (leftward)
    await user.click(screen.getByText("Produk"));

    const panel = screen.getByTestId("product-panel").parentElement;

    // Before rAF: no slide
    expect(panel?.className).not.toContain("slide");   // <-- FAILS
    // className IS "h-full overflow-hidden animate-tab-slide-right"

    // After rAF: slide-left
    flushRaf();
    expect(panel?.className).toContain("slide");
    expect(panel?.className).toContain("left");
  });

  it("schedules rAF on every tab change (including first)", async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup();
    const CatalogPage = (await import("../index")).default;
    render(() => <CatalogPage />);

    const rafCountBefore = rafCallbacks.length;        // captured AFTER initial render (= 1)

    // First tab change: produk → varian
    await user.click(screen.getByText("Varian"));
    expect(rafCallbacks.length).toBeGreaterThan(rafCountBefore);  // <-- FAILS (1 > 1 false)
  });
});
```

### 4. Kobalte Tabs component (src/components/ui/tabs.tsx) — for context
Uses `@kobalte/core/tabs`. `Tabs = TabsPrimitive.Root`, `TabsList`, `TabsTrigger` (with `data-[selected]`), `TabsContent`, `TabsIndicator` (absolute positioned bar). Kobalte's `TabsIndicator` internally uses `@solid-primitives/resize-observer` to track the selected trigger.

## What We Have Tried

1. **Reactive `cn()` class binding (no rAF).** The animation class was applied directly via:
   ```tsx
   class={cn("h-full overflow-hidden", direction() === "right" ? "animate-tab-slide-right" : "animate-tab-slide-left")}
   ```
   Inside `<Show keyed when={tab()}>`. Result: in the real browser, the FIRST tab change produced NO visible animation, but the second and later changes animated correctly. The class was present in the DOM (tests that only checked class presence passed), but the browser didn't animate it because the keyed remount created the element already-animating, so no "from→to" transition was observed by the compositor.

2. **Two-phase via Solid `use:slideIn` custom directive** that called `requestAnimationFrame` to add the class. Tests failed the same way as the current `ref` version (below).

3. **Two-phase via `ref={applySlide}` callback** (current code) that reads `direction()` and calls `requestAnimationFrame(() => el.classList.add(...))`. Test results:
   - Test 1 (produk→varian, check class absent before flushRaf, present after): **PASSES.**
   - Test 2 (kategori→produk, check class absent before flushRaf): **FAILS** — className is already `"h-full overflow-hidden animate-tab-slide-right"` BEFORE flushRaf, and it says `slide-right` even though direction should be `left`.
   - Test 3 (rAF count should increase on first change): **FAILS** — `rafCallbacks.length` stays at 1, implying the ref callback did NOT fire (or did not call rAF) when the keyed Show remounted.

4. **Indicator positioning bug earlier** (now fixed): `TabsIndicator` was a sibling of `TabsList`; moving it inside `TabsList` and making the list `relative` fixed the indicator offset.

5. **Considered SSGOI page transitions** instead of CSS — the user prefers to keep this as in-page tab animation, not full route transitions, if feasible.

## The Likely Culprit(s)

My leading hypotheses, in order:

1. **SolidJS `Show keyed` + `ref` callback re-invocation semantics.** With `keyed`, SolidJS destroys and recreates the child on value change, which *should* re-invoke `ref`. But Test 3 shows the rAF count not increasing, suggesting the ref callback is NOT being re-invoked on the second remount in the jsdom test environment — OR it is invoked but `direction()` is read at a point where the signal hasn't updated yet, so it adds the wrong class. There may be a reactivity ordering issue: `setDirection` + `setTab` in the same `handleTabChange` batch, but `direction()` is read inside the `ref`/`applySlide` closure that runs during the render triggered by `setTab` — does SolidJS guarantee `direction` is already updated when the ref fires?

2. **`direction()` stale read inside the ref callback.** In Test 2 the resulting class is `slide-right` when it should be `slide-left`. This points to `direction()` returning the OLD value ("right") at the moment `applySlide` runs, even though `setDirection` was called in `handleTabChange` before `setTab`. SolidJS batches signal writes; the read inside the ref callback may execute before the batch flushes `direction`. This is the strongest single hypothesis.

3. **jsdom rAF timing differences from real browsers.** The mocked rAF captures without running, but other libraries (Kobalte, corvu, `@solid-primitives/resize-observer`) also call rAF during the same render, and my `rafCallbacks` array mixes theirs with mine — making the count assertions unreliable. The "class already present before flushRaf" in Test 2 might actually be a leftover class from a *previous* element that was not properly unmounted, OR a rAF from the previous flushRaf() that targeted the wrong element.

4. **The underlying first-animation-skip bug may be independent of the test failures** — i.e., the real-browser bug and the test failures might have different root causes. The test failures could be masking whether the two-phase fix actually works in a browser.

## Research Questions

Please answer, with references to SolidJS source/docs/behavior:

1. **SolidJS keyed `Show` + `ref` re-invocation:** When `<Show keyed when={x()}>` re-mounts its child on a value change, is the `ref` callback on a descendant element guaranteed to re-fire? Are there cases (jsdom, same-function-identity refs, etc.) where it does NOT re-fire? What is the canonical SolidJS pattern for running a side effect every time a keyed element is (re)created?

2. **Signal batching & read-during-render ordering:** When two signals are set in the same synchronous handler (`setDirection(...)` then `setTab(...)`), and one of them (`setTab`) triggers a keyed remount whose `ref`/directive reads the OTHER signal (`direction()`), is the read guaranteed to see the NEW value of `direction`? Does SolidJS flush the first setter before rendering the effect of the second? If not, what is the correct ordering or pattern (e.g., read direction from a separate non-batched source, combine into one signal, use `onMount`/`createEffect` instead of a ref)?

3. **Best practice for "animate on every mount" in SolidJS:** What is the idiomatic, reliable way to run a CSS (or Web Animations API) entrance animation every time a SolidJS element is (re)mounted — including the very first mount after initial render? Specifically for the case where you need the browser to observe a class/state change after the element exists. Is a single `requestAnimationFrame` sufficient, or is a double-rAF (two nested frames) required? Are there SolidJS-specific gotchas (e.g., `<Dynamic>`, portals, `batch`) that interfere?

4. **Is CSS keyframe animation even the right tool here,** or should I use the Web Animations API (`element.animate(...)`) for guaranteed re-trigger on every mount? What are the trade-offs (WAAPI vs CSS class toggle) for an entrance slide that must replay on every keyed remount?

5. **Testing approach:** How do you reliably unit-test a "mount clean, then rAF adds animation class" two-phase behavior in jsdom given that multiple libraries schedule rAFs into the same frame? Should the test instead use `@testing-library` `waitFor`/`findBy` with real timers, or assert on `getAnimations()`/computed style rather than className? What does SolidJS testing library (`@solidjs/testing-library`) recommend for animation assertions?

6. **Kobalte Tabs `onChange` + controlled `value`:** With Kobalte's `Tabs` controlled via `value={tab()} onChange={handleTabChange}`, is there any async timing in `onChange` (e.g., it fires after Kobalte's internal state settles) that could cause my `handleTabChange` to run out-of-order relative to the keyed `Show` remount? Is `onChange` synchronous in the same microtask as the click?

Please provide concrete code recommendations (SolidJS idiomatic) for the catalog page, and a corrected test strategy. Prioritize explaining the `direction()` stale-read hypothesis (#2) and the ref-not-reinvoked hypothesis (#1), since the test evidence (class is `slide-right` when `slide-left` expected; rAF count not increasing) most directly implicates those.
